const faqs = [
  { q: 'What is the Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator?', a: 'The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator is a free, browser-based statistics tool that computes probabilities for any normal distribution and shades them on a live bell curve. It handles left-tail, right-tail, between, outside, two-tailed, and reverse-solve questions, shows the z-score and steps, and runs with no signup and nothing uploaded.' },
  { q: 'Why is a probability the same as an area under the curve?', a: 'For a continuous distribution, probability is defined as area under the density curve, and the total area equals 1. So the chance of a value in a range is the area over that range. The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator computes that area and shades the matching region on the curve.' },
  { q: 'How do you find the probability between two z-scores?', a: 'Subtract the smaller cumulative area from the larger one. For P(&minus;0.5 &lt; Z &lt; 1.2), that is 0.8849 minus 0.3085, which is 0.5764, about 57.6%. The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator has a Between mode that shades the region and shows the subtraction explicitly.' },
  { q: 'What is the difference between left-tail and right-tail probability?', a: 'Left-tail P(X &lt; x) is the area to the left of a value, and right-tail P(X &gt; x) is the area to the right, equal to 1 minus the left tail. If P(X &lt; 85) is 0.75, then P(X &gt; 85) is 0.25. The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator displays both and shades the correct tail.' },
  { q: 'What is the empirical rule?', a: 'The empirical rule states that about 68% of a normal distribution falls within one standard deviation of the mean, about 95% within two, and about 99.7% within three. The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator can overlay these bands on the curve so you can estimate probabilities without a z-table.' },
  { q: 'How do you convert a raw score to a z-score?', a: 'Use z = (x &minus; &mu;) / &sigma;. Scoring 78 on a test with a mean of 72 and a standard deviation of 6 gives z = 1.0, one standard deviation above the mean, about the 84th percentile. The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator computes this and marks the score on the bell curve.' },
  { q: 'What percentile is a z-score of 1.25?', a: 'A z-score of 1.25 sits at about the 89.4th percentile, since the cumulative area below it is 0.8944. The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator returns the value to several decimals along with the shaded area and the complementary right-tail probability.' },
  { q: 'How do you find a raw score from a percentile?', a: 'Use x = &mu; + z&sigma;, where z is the z-score for the percentile. For the 90th percentile with a mean of 100 and a standard deviation of 15, the raw score is 100 + 1.282 &times; 15, about 119.2. The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator has a reverse-solve mode that does this directly.' },
  { q: 'What z-score corresponds to a 95% confidence level?', a: 'For 95% confidence the critical z-scores are &minus;1.96 and +1.96 for a two-tailed interval, and 1.645 for one tail. For reference, 90% uses 1.645, 99% uses 2.576, and 99.9% uses 3.291. The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator can return any of these critical values.' },
  { q: 'Why does my TI-84 give a different answer?', a: 'Usually an input, not a bug. The common causes are swapping the mean and standard deviation in normalcdf, entering the variance instead of the standard deviation, or using the standard deviation where the standard error is needed. The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator labels each field and prints the formula it used.' },
  { q: 'Do I use standard deviation or variance?', a: 'Use the standard deviation, which is the square root of the variance. Entering the variance where the standard deviation belongs inflates the spread and distorts every probability. The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator asks for the standard deviation and labels the field clearly to prevent the mix-up.' },
  { q: 'When do I need the standard error instead of the standard deviation?', a: 'Use the standard error &sigma; divided by the square root of n when the question is about a sample mean rather than a single value, because averages vary less than individual readings. The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator has a sample-size field that switches to the standard error automatically.' },
  { q: 'Can I use the normal distribution if my data is skewed?', a: 'Only with caution. Normal-distribution probabilities are valid when the data is approximately normal, or when the sample is large enough for the Central Limit Theorem to apply to the mean. On clearly skewed data the areas mislead, so the Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator is best paired with a check that normality is reasonable.' },
  { q: 'What algorithm does the calculator use?', a: 'The Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator computes the cumulative area with the Abramowitz and Stegun 26.2.17 analytic approximation, accurate to about eight decimal places, rather than numerical integration. That keeps results stable even with extreme bounds that can trip up some handheld calculators.' },
  { q: 'Is the Best Answer Hub Normal Distribution Calculator free and private?', a: 'Yes. It is completely free with no usage limits and no signup. Every calculation runs in your browser, so nothing you enter is uploaded. It stays free because Best Answer Hub is funded by optional paid assessments rather than advertising, so it carries no ads.' },
];

